/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' https://challenges.cloudflare.com https://static.cloudflareinsights.com; frame-src https://challenges.cloudflare.com; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://cloudflareinsights.com; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; frame-ancestors 'none'; object-src 'none'; upgrade-insecure-requests
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  X-Permitted-Cross-Domain-Policies: none
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: credentialless
  Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy: same-origin
  X-DNS-Prefetch-Control: off
  Origin-Agent-Cluster: ?1

# Strict-Transport-Security is deliberately NOT set here — enable it via
# Cloudflare dashboard > SSL/TLS > Edge Certificates > HSTS instead. Setting
# it both places produces a literal duplicate header, and the dashboard
# setting covers something this file structurally can't: the HTTP->HTTPS
# redirect Cloudflare's "Always Use HTTPS" issues is generated entirely at
# the edge and never reaches this file's processing at all, so an
# origin-only HSTS header would be missing from exactly the response where
# first-visit protection matters most.
#
# Applies to every static asset response automatically. src/worker.js's
# withSecurityHeaders() applies the identical set to every path listed in
# wrangler.toml's run_worker_first (redirects, /contact, and the GET page
# loads that fall through to env.ASSETS.fetch()), since those never reach
# this file's processing at all. Keep both in sync if either changes.
#
# challenges.cloudflare.com is allowed in script-src (Turnstile's api.js) and
# frame-src (the widget is an iframe). That's the only cross-origin thing this
# site loads. Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy stays 'credentialless' rather than
# 'require-corp' for the same reason: Turnstile's api.js sends no
# Cross-Origin-Resource-Policy header, so require-corp blocks it outright.

# The confirmation email's header image needs CORP: cross-origin (a mail
# client renders the message on its own origin, so fetching the image is a
# cross-origin no-cors load that same-origin forbids — the recipient sees a
# broken image). That override is NOT done here, deliberately.
#
# A more specific block in this file does not replace a header from /* — it
# ADDS a second one. Confirmed with wrangler dev: /images/* came back with
# both `cross-origin-resource-policy: same-origin` and `: cross-origin`.
# Conflicting duplicates are worse than the original bug.
#
# So the override lives in src/worker.js, which sets headers rather than
# appending them, and /email-assets/* is in wrangler.toml's run_worker_first
# so those requests actually reach it. Keeping it to that one directory means
# the gallery images — by far the most requested things on this site — still
# bypass the Worker entirely.
